<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amalric I of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136–<a href="/years/1174">1174</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Amalric of Jerusalem</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amalric I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1163 to 1174. Before his accession he held the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>. As king he led repeated invasions of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the 1160s, competing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who ended up controlling Egypt. He was succeeded by his son Baldwin IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Amalric I of Jerusalem" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amalric_receives_a_message_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amalric was the younger son of King Fulk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Melisende</a>. After Fulk died in 1143 following a horse-riding accident, Melisende was crowned again alongside her elder son, the 13-year-old Baldwin III, and later withheld power from him. In 1151 she gave Amalric the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, and he became her most important partisan besides the Church. In 1152 Baldwin besieged her and her loyal men, Amalric among them, in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and deposed her. Baldwin conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> from Fatimid Egypt in 1153 and later granted both Ascalon and Jaffa to Amalric. In 1157 Amalric married Agnes of Courtenay, and they had a daughter, Sibylla, and a son, Baldwin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Baldwin III died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> on 10 February 1163 without children. The High Court refused to recognise Amalric as king unless he repudiated Agnes, and he accepted; the marriage was annulled on grounds of kinship, and the papal legate was present. The children's legitimacy was confirmed. The patriarch crowned him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> on 18 February, the day of Baldwin's funeral. Early in his reign he passed the Assise sur la ligece, which let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of powerful lords appeal directly to the king.</p><p>His chief aim was the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, whose government was in disarray as the viziers Dirgham and Shawar fought for power. If <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>, who had united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, took Egypt too, the crusader states would be encircled. After a first expedition in 1163, he supported Shawar against Dirgham and Nur al-Din's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> in 1164. Amalric invaded again in 1167–68, but Egypt fell to Shirkuh instead. Shirkuh died in 1169, and Amalric launched a joint attack on Damietta with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, whose grandniece Maria became his second wife. It failed amid rain, shortage of food and poor cooperation, and Nur al-Din used Amalric's absences to plunder the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amalric's son Baldwin showed signs of leprosy in childhood, and William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, his tutor, found that the boy did not feel pain in his right arm. Amalric sought a capable son-in-law and invited Count Stephen I of Sancerre to marry Sibylla, but for unknown reasons Stephen refused and left. In 1174, while trying to profit from the confusion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s death, Amalric caught dysentery and died on 11 July. His son succeeded him as Baldwin IV.</p><p>William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> described him as serious and taciturn, intellectually gifted, daring in battle and fond of money. The Muslim chronicler Ibn al-Athir called him the bravest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> kings. One historian argues that acting without his Byzantine allies in 1168 may have allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to be united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amalric of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom won important victories, including King Baldwin IV’s defeat of Saladin at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177, and King <b>Amalric</b> I invaded Egypt several times between 1163 and 1169, briefly forcing it to pay tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1163 Nur ad-Din contested Egypt with <b>Amalric of Jerusalem</b>: Shirkuh, with his nephew Saladin, won the Battle of al-Babein in 1167, relieved Cairo in 1168 and in 1169 killed the vizier Shawar and seized Egypt for Nur ad-Din.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin III captured Ascalon from the Fatimids in 1153, and the combined county was given to his brother <b>Amalric</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1160" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Amalric I of Jerusalem"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 4839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 4839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1160">What was here in 1160? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
